TI  - Ibrutinib and tracheal mucormycosis: A case report and systematic review of 
      literature.
PG  - 101414
LID - S1156-5233(23)00058-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.mycmed.2023.101414 [doi]
AB  - Ibrutinib, a Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitor, has been approved for 
      various hematological malignancies. Invasive aspergillosis is a known 
      complication of ibrutinib, but mucormycosis is rare. We describe the case of a 
      70-year-old man with mantle cell lymphoma infiltrating the trachea, managed with 
      a tracheobronchial stent and ibrutinib. He had improved one month after 
      treatment, and we removed the airway stent. Four months later, he developed 
      tracheal nodules confirmed to be tracheal mucormycosis and responded to liposomal 
      amphotericin B (3.5 g) followed by posaconazole. After transient improvement, the 
      tracheal lesions recurred, the biopsy showed lymphoma (with no evidence of 
      mucormycosis), and he died. A systematic review of the literature identified 20 
      additional cases of ibrutinib-associated mucormycosis. Most of the 21 patients 
      included were men (95%), and ibrutinib was the only risk factor in 15.7%. The 
      reported mortality was 31.6% (6/19), attributable to mucormycosis in half the 
      cases.
